1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
A drill bit leads to the disappearance of an entire lake and everything in it.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:12,000
The tallest man-made structures in the world turn to crumpled steel.

3
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:16,000
A poorly designed dam wipes out an entire community in minutes.

4
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:19,000
A freeway collapse in earthquake country.

5
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:22,000
And the worst oil spill in US history.

6
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:26,000
Now, Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:41,900
Architects and engineers have long been obsessed with the idea of building the tallest structure

8
00:00:41,901 --> 00:00:44,060
in the world.

9
00:00:44,061 --> 00:00:49,600
From the ancient pyramid of Khufu, which stood as man's highest creation for 43 centuries,

10
00:00:49,601 --> 00:00:56,120
before being eclipsed by the Eiffel Tower, to the 20th century's skyscrapers, which

11
00:00:56,121 --> 00:00:59,144
jostle for top rank as they push ever higher.

12
00:00:59,145 --> 00:01:01,900
The desire to be on top, looking down, is

13
00:01:01,901 --> 00:01:03,088
an enduring one.

14
00:01:03,089 --> 00:01:08,580
But all these magnificent structures fall far short of a stark utilitarian

15
00:01:08,581 --> 00:01:15,580
tower that rises up from the plains of North Dakota to a vertiginous height of 2,063 feet.

16
00:01:15,581 --> 00:01:19,017
Meet the KVLY Broadcasting Tower.

17
00:01:19,018 --> 00:01:24,640
Its antenna stands 356 feet higher than the tip of the

18
00:01:24,641 --> 00:01:31,500
antenna on the Sears Tower, higher than any other structure on Earth.

19
00:01:31,501 --> 00:01:35,640
They are very impressive structures and yet when you drive by them they don't look like

20
00:01:35,641 --> 00:01:39,450
much. In fact they're even hard to see under certain conditions.

21
00:01:39,451 --> 00:01:40,700
But there is an awful

22
00:01:40,701 --> 00:01:47,400
lot of very sophisticated engineering that goes into designing them.

23
00:01:47,401 --> 00:01:52,880
FM and television waves can't go around obstructions and thus require a very high launching pad

24
00:01:52,881 --> 00:01:55,480
to reach the desired coverage area.

25
00:01:55,481 --> 00:01:59,000
In regions of the Midwest and South lacking tall mountains,

26
00:01:59,001 --> 00:02:02,280
the solution is to build a tower of extreme height,

27
00:02:02,281 --> 00:02:03,700
like the KVLY tower.

28
00:02:08,680 --> 00:02:12,240
The most characteristic design feature of broadcast towers

29
00:02:12,241 --> 00:02:14,440
is this steel lattice framework, which

30
00:02:14,441 --> 00:02:17,800
is bolted together in 20 to 40 foot sections all the way

31
00:02:17,801 --> 00:02:20,280
to the top.

32
00:02:20,281 --> 00:02:26,100
A less noticeable but crucial part of the tallest towers are thin steel cables called guy lines.

33
00:02:27,280 --> 00:02:32,280
These high-strength pre-tensioned cables stretch down from the tower at several vertical points

34
00:02:32,281 --> 00:02:35,120
and are anchored into cement foundations on the ground.

35
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:38,440
Basically, a guy cable is a tension element.

36
00:02:38,900 --> 00:02:42,300
The only force that it sees is tension. It can't take any other force.

37
00:02:42,301 --> 00:02:45,600
The guy cables are not carrying any gravity loads or any weight.

38
00:02:45,900 --> 00:02:49,820
They're only resisting the tendency for the wind to push the tower over.

39
00:02:50,280 --> 00:02:58,160
Since they anchor the tower to the ground, guy lines prevent antenna towers from being considered the highest freestanding structures in the world.

40
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:02,480
They can also play a key role in a tower's collapse.

41
00:03:04,100 --> 00:03:11,780
This is the view from an antenna tower in Missouri City, Texas, on the final day of its construction, December 7, 1982.

42
00:03:11,781 --> 00:03:16,280
known simply as the senior road tower

43
00:03:16,281 --> 00:03:19,900
it was to be 1,971 feet high

44
00:03:19,901 --> 00:03:24,840
less than 100 feet kept it from being the tallest structure in the world

45
00:03:24,841 --> 00:03:27,600
an hour after these pictures were taken

46
00:03:27,601 --> 00:03:30,760
it would be reduced to a crumpled mass of steel

47
00:03:30,761 --> 00:03:33,800
the basic tower structure was complete

48
00:03:33,801 --> 00:03:37,680
and the first portion of the antenna had been installed

49
00:03:37,681 --> 00:03:41,340
the antenna was a very large broadband antenna

50
00:03:41,341 --> 00:03:43,940
for multiple FM stations.

51
00:03:44,340 --> 00:03:46,140
It was built in two sections,

52
00:03:46,880 --> 00:03:49,640
each of which was about 60 feet long.

53
00:03:50,460 --> 00:03:53,380
And they were lifting the final section,

54
00:03:54,140 --> 00:03:57,500
and it was moving up the tower about halfway.

55
00:03:58,640 --> 00:04:01,140
The FM antenna weighed six tons.

56
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:04,860
The five-man rigging crew had constructed a hoisting device

57
00:04:04,861 --> 00:04:07,640
called a gin pole, specifically for this job.

58
00:04:07,641 --> 00:04:09,280
When you are building a tower,

59
00:04:09,281 --> 00:04:11,820
you typically cannot reach it with a crane.

60
00:04:12,100 --> 00:04:14,860
So if this is a tower, we hoist a gin pole up the side of it

61
00:04:14,861 --> 00:04:16,320
and we put it up higher than the tower.

62
00:04:16,760 --> 00:04:19,580
Now from the top of that, we can put something on the top of the tower.

63
00:04:20,760 --> 00:04:22,120
In a dangerous maneuver,

64
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:26,560
three of the five men were riding inside the antenna as it was being lifted,

65
00:04:27,020 --> 00:04:29,180
while the other two were on the tower itself.

66
00:04:29,800 --> 00:04:32,060
You're mostly focused on the work that you have to do

67
00:04:32,061 --> 00:04:35,020
and the task at hand and staying alive and not falling off.

68
00:04:36,240 --> 00:04:38,340
At about the 1,500-foot level,

69
00:04:38,940 --> 00:04:42,220
The U-bolts on the clamp bearing the weight of the antenna failed.

70
00:04:42,840 --> 00:04:45,980
The antenna and the men attached to it fell toward the ground.

71
00:04:46,160 --> 00:04:47,580
Oh, my God.

72
00:04:52,060 --> 00:04:57,400
At 1,000 feet, the antenna sliced one of the 27 guy lines that held up the tower.

73
00:04:58,140 --> 00:05:04,600
The tower sprang in one direction, whipped back the opposite way, and collapsed with a thunderous roar.

74
00:05:04,601 --> 00:05:13,600
Say that one of those cables had a tension in it of, I'll say, 20,000 pounds,

75
00:05:14,100 --> 00:05:16,800
and then you suddenly break that.

76
00:05:17,380 --> 00:05:20,220
So you release that kind of energy.

77
00:05:21,560 --> 00:05:26,500
The other cables that are still intact are pulling by that amount of force the other direction.

78
00:05:27,060 --> 00:05:30,680
And so they pull the tower sideways, and that causes it to buckle.

79
00:05:30,681 --> 00:05:34,960
The stunned contractors ran to the scene of destruction.

80
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:37,680
All five men were dead.

81
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:40,760
Does anybody hear this stuff?

82
00:05:45,440 --> 00:05:51,140
The transformation from sky-high tower to tangle of steel took 17 seconds.

83
00:05:53,840 --> 00:05:58,840
The total collapse of the senior road tower was caused by the guyline snapping.

84
00:05:59,800 --> 00:06:05,580
But the disaster was set in motion by the failure of the half-inch diameter U-bolts on the gin pole.

85
00:06:06,900 --> 00:06:13,200
During the subsequent investigation, analysis would show that the sheer strength of the bolts was half what it should have been.

86
00:06:14,320 --> 00:06:20,300
Some observers believe these small puffs of smoke are actually debris flying off the bolts as they snapped.

87
00:06:22,020 --> 00:06:28,360
According to the investigation, the riggers had not consulted with a licensed engineer when selecting the U-bolts.

88
00:06:28,840 --> 00:06:35,080
Tall antenna towers and the brave men who work on them are still at risk during construction

89
00:06:35,081 --> 00:06:36,900
and maintenance.

90
00:06:36,901 --> 00:06:45,560
Between 1992 and 2002, 163 people died in tower accidents.

91
00:06:45,561 --> 00:06:50,180
For engineers, the challenge is to reduce the risk of catastrophic failure during moments

92
00:06:50,181 --> 00:06:53,180
when the forces acting on the tower are being shifted.

93
00:06:53,181 --> 00:06:56,800
For instance, if they're replacing a brace, what happens is they go up there and they

94
00:06:56,801 --> 00:06:58,620
take that brace off.

95
00:06:58,621 --> 00:07:03,460
Once they take the brace off the tower, now the tower is vulnerable.

96
00:07:03,461 --> 00:07:05,780
Natural forces also pose a threat.

97
00:07:05,781 --> 00:07:09,940
Ice and wind, especially acting together, can bring a tower down.

98
00:07:09,941 --> 00:07:14,420
So you have the weight of the ice itself, and then you have the increased area that's

99
00:07:14,421 --> 00:07:18,600
exposed to the wind due to the thickness of the ice coatings.

100
00:07:18,601 --> 00:07:22,800
And this can increase the wind forces considerably.

101
00:07:22,801 --> 00:07:28,620
But it's the collapses during routine construction and maintenance that continue

102
00:07:28,621 --> 00:07:32,280
to echo lessons not learned from the tragic senior road tower disaster.

103
00:07:33,360 --> 00:07:38,060
These collapses, which most often involve the loss of human life, are the most preventable.

104
00:07:38,600 --> 00:07:43,620
It is an industry that does have some hazards to it during construction, and you certainly

105
00:07:43,621 --> 00:07:45,200
have to be extremely careful.

106
00:07:45,860 --> 00:07:53,180
And there has been a big increase in the attention being paid to the safety issues during construction.

107
00:07:53,181 --> 00:08:00,860
Which is good, because humanity's hunger for instant information, communication, and entertainment is only increasing.

108
00:08:02,560 --> 00:08:08,920
Communications off of towers and mountaintops and broadcast sites is the backbone of our entire society.

109
00:08:09,080 --> 00:08:15,980
With all of our communications, police, fire, ambulance, television, radio, two-way radio communications,

110
00:08:16,220 --> 00:08:21,380
all sorts of communications are being handled by these tower sites that you don't even realize.

111
00:08:21,381 --> 00:08:26,600
Another vital resource moves all around us 24 hours a day.

112
00:08:27,620 --> 00:08:28,320
Oil.

113
00:08:28,540 --> 00:08:31,840
We don't think about it until disaster strikes.

114
00:08:33,080 --> 00:08:38,300
If a baseball were dropped from the KVLY tower in North Dakota at the beginning of this sentence,

115
00:08:38,820 --> 00:08:42,640
it wouldn't hit the ground until six seconds into the next commercial.

116
00:08:45,420 --> 00:08:48,640
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

117
00:08:48,641 --> 00:08:53,640
We now return to Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

118
00:08:55,280 --> 00:08:59,620
It grabbed front page headlines and filled television news time for months.

119
00:09:00,320 --> 00:09:08,160
The images of dead and dying wildlife and hundreds of miles of pristine coastline choking with crude oil saddened and angered the public.

120
00:09:09,360 --> 00:09:17,480
Another image was burned into the public's consciousness as the media focused on an allegedly drunken captain who had steered his boat into a reef.

121
00:09:18,060 --> 00:09:19,460
But there's a lot more to the story.

122
00:09:20,040 --> 00:09:22,937
The Exxon Valdez oil spill, the worst in U.S.

123
00:09:22,938 --> 00:09:25,640
history, was also an engineering disaster.

124
00:09:25,900 --> 00:09:30,500
The human element in the Exxon Valdez is multidimensional,

125
00:09:30,800 --> 00:09:34,560
and people focused again on the simplistic issue of Captain Hazelwood.

126
00:09:34,920 --> 00:09:40,420
We like to be able to point the failure at one person or one thing,

127
00:09:40,680 --> 00:09:44,860
and if Joe Hazelwood had not had that last drink, everything would have been okay.

128
00:09:44,861 --> 00:09:48,980
and not seeing the complexity of the incident.

129
00:09:51,680 --> 00:09:54,740
We're approaching the Exxon Valdez.

130
00:09:54,900 --> 00:09:55,840
She's hard to ground.

131
00:09:56,340 --> 00:09:59,720
She's got the starboard slop tag, starboard wing tank,

132
00:09:59,880 --> 00:10:02,140
and possibly five center are impacted.

133
00:10:04,040 --> 00:10:07,900
The grounding of the tanker Exxon Valdez on Bly Reef

134
00:10:07,901 --> 00:10:12,060
occurred at four minutes after midnight on March 24, 1989,

135
00:10:12,860 --> 00:10:14,580
a clear night with calm seas.

136
00:10:14,860 --> 00:10:17,380
You can see a black oil line right here.

137
00:10:18,460 --> 00:10:22,500
The vessel has lost 105,000 barrels of oil so far.

138
00:10:24,200 --> 00:10:28,260
The massive tanker, nearly as long as the Chrysler building is tall,

139
00:10:28,760 --> 00:10:32,720
was carrying 53 million gallons of North Slope Alaskan crude.

140
00:10:33,700 --> 00:10:37,760
Oil from the North Slope makes an 800-mile journey through the Trans-Alaska pipeline

141
00:10:37,761 --> 00:10:41,260
to the port of Valdez, where it's pumped into tankers.

142
00:10:41,261 --> 00:10:48,640
the north slope oil and the pipeline to valdez and the sea leg from valdez to the west coast

143
00:10:48,641 --> 00:10:54,220
of the u.s was a highly political decision when it was created you may remember that the bill to

144
00:10:54,221 --> 00:11:01,520
to create the the pipeline was was passed on a tie vote the immediate cause of the accident was

145
00:11:01,521 --> 00:11:06,620
fairly simple captain joseph hazelwood had ordered the ship to leave the outbound shipping lane

146
00:11:06,621 --> 00:11:11,460
and head inward to avoid icebergs, a fairly common move in Prince William's sound.

147
00:11:13,080 --> 00:11:20,400
Hazelwood then left the third mate, who had steered super tankers through the sound 25 times previously, in command of the ship.

148
00:11:21,580 --> 00:11:28,060
To re-enter the shipping lanes before running into Bly Reef, the Exxon Valdez would have to make a hard right turn.

149
00:11:28,540 --> 00:11:32,780
This is not a sports car you're driving. This is a 200,000 deadweight ton tanker.

150
00:11:32,781 --> 00:11:38,040
and when you give a rudder command or an engine command, it doesn't instantly change.

151
00:11:38,460 --> 00:11:42,700
The third mate had a relatively inexperienced seaman assisting him with a turn.

152
00:11:43,340 --> 00:11:46,760
The 987-foot tanker failed to make the turn in time.

153
00:11:47,600 --> 00:11:53,300
Traveling at 12 knots, the ship slammed into the rocky bottom of Bly Reef and ran full of ground.

154
00:11:54,160 --> 00:11:56,340
Eight of its 11 cargo tanks were torn open.

155
00:11:57,080 --> 00:11:58,780
Oil gushed freely into the sea.

156
00:11:59,540 --> 00:12:01,440
11 million gallons would enter the water.

157
00:12:02,380 --> 00:12:03,740
In his call to the Coast Guard,

158
00:12:04,180 --> 00:12:06,820
Hazelwood made one of the great understatements of all time.

159
00:12:08,880 --> 00:12:10,480
There should be any radar there.

160
00:12:10,481 --> 00:12:15,580
We've fetched up hard ground north of Goose Island,

161
00:12:15,800 --> 00:12:19,300
a fly reef, and at that point,

162
00:12:20,060 --> 00:12:21,180
we'll get some oil.

163
00:12:22,940 --> 00:12:24,940
Human error had played its part,

164
00:12:25,520 --> 00:12:29,160
but the ensuing disaster would reveal a fatally linked chain of engineering

165
00:12:29,161 --> 00:12:30,620
as well as human failure.

166
00:12:31,440 --> 00:12:34,020
The most immediate failure was with the ship itself.

167
00:12:34,021 --> 00:12:37,900
When the Exxon Valdez happened in 1989, when that ran aground,

168
00:12:38,260 --> 00:12:43,760
the whole issue of these monster ships that carry these massive amounts of oil,

169
00:12:44,400 --> 00:12:48,660
the issue of the ship having only a single hull came to light.

170
00:12:49,880 --> 00:12:52,700
The Exxon Valdez was only two years old.

171
00:12:53,160 --> 00:12:57,680
Yet only three quarters of an inch of steel was all that stood between millions of gallons of oil

172
00:12:57,681 --> 00:13:01,320
and one of the most environmentally sensitive areas of the United States.

173
00:13:01,440 --> 00:13:07,500
The long-awaited catastrophe of the single-hulled supertanker had arrived.

174
00:13:07,840 --> 00:13:12,360
When the controversial Trans-Alaska pipeline began operations in 1977,

175
00:13:13,220 --> 00:13:17,280
it came with promises that all tankers would be outfitted with double hulls.

176
00:13:17,500 --> 00:13:20,580
Think of a U for a cross-section of a supertanker.

177
00:13:21,020 --> 00:13:25,100
Then think of a smaller U inside with a 10-foot difference in between.

178
00:13:25,800 --> 00:13:28,860
So it's basically a hull inside a hull. That's why they call it a double hull.

179
00:13:29,420 --> 00:13:38,320
And so if there is an impact, the energy of that impact hopefully is taken up by penetrating the first hull, but not the second one.

180
00:13:39,220 --> 00:13:42,820
The double hull was a promise not kept or enforced.

181
00:13:43,640 --> 00:13:48,200
In 1989, nearly all of the tankers leaving the port of Valdez had a single hull.

182
00:13:50,900 --> 00:13:56,060
But the ship itself was only part of a complex oil transport system that had broken down.

183
00:13:57,500 --> 00:13:58,520
A systems failure.

184
00:13:58,860 --> 00:14:04,520
There were assurances given by the oil companies that it would be operated in a fail-safe mode.

185
00:14:05,420 --> 00:14:14,360
And sometime between 1977 and 1989, the operations of the system had degraded to a point where a very severe accident could occur.

186
00:14:14,361 --> 00:14:21,020
When you get to a system where one mistake means that you lost the whole thing, then you really haven't designed the system very well.

187
00:14:22,620 --> 00:14:25,960
A key part of the system's failure was inadequate radar.

188
00:14:26,940 --> 00:14:32,180
Shortly before the disaster, the Valdez Coast Guard Station's radar crews had been cut in half,

189
00:14:32,760 --> 00:14:37,680
and the radar itself had been replaced with a unit that was no longer capable of detecting Bly Reef.

190
00:14:38,260 --> 00:14:45,580
So when the Exxon Valdez was heading for Bly Reef, it was not under any external radar coverage.

191
00:14:46,960 --> 00:14:52,060
Reductions in the tanker's crew size, nearly 50%, further increased the chance of disaster.

192
00:14:52,061 --> 00:14:57,700
The final weak link in the chain was the spill response.

193
00:14:58,240 --> 00:15:02,940
The strategy was if you have a major oil spill to contain it near the ship to minimize damage

194
00:15:02,941 --> 00:15:05,320
and then remove it from the containment,

195
00:15:05,940 --> 00:15:10,420
the response was a total failure because the oil totally escaped from the vicinity of the ship.

196
00:15:12,780 --> 00:15:18,860
Nearly every technology used to clean up the oil spill arrived too late and was ineffective.

197
00:15:19,780 --> 00:15:23,980
Many of the containment booms, large floating curtains used to corral the oil,

198
00:15:24,460 --> 00:15:26,200
were of the wrong type for ocean use.

199
00:15:27,020 --> 00:15:28,340
Some actually sank.

200
00:15:29,160 --> 00:15:32,620
Fishermen with buckets reportedly had better success in scooping up oil

201
00:15:32,621 --> 00:15:37,060
than Exxon did with skimmers, mechanical vacuums that suck up oil.

202
00:15:37,780 --> 00:15:40,340
And when foul weather arrived on the third day,

203
00:15:40,740 --> 00:15:43,300
whipping up the oil into a thick moose-like froth,

204
00:15:43,900 --> 00:15:48,080
it was too late for chemical dispersants to be effective in breaking down the oil.

205
00:15:48,860 --> 00:15:52,840
The initial cleanup was a total failure because there was inadequate resources.

206
00:15:53,700 --> 00:15:56,080
The people of Alaska justifiably felt betrayed.

207
00:15:56,700 --> 00:16:01,480
They were told that if anything happens, we have the equipment, we have the skills to clean it up.

208
00:16:01,620 --> 00:16:02,840
And that was not there.

209
00:16:03,520 --> 00:16:04,600
It's a pristine region.

210
00:16:04,740 --> 00:16:07,300
If you've ever been there, Prince William Sound is incredibly beautiful.

211
00:16:07,840 --> 00:16:12,060
So the environmental damage was proportionally very high for the amount of oil spilled.

212
00:16:12,061 --> 00:16:19,060
then it just became just a massive shoreline cleanup and restoration exercise,

213
00:16:19,960 --> 00:16:22,220
which took months and it cost a lot of money,

214
00:16:22,600 --> 00:16:25,340
and it's still doubtful what good that actually did.

215
00:16:29,240 --> 00:16:33,840
The good that did come out of this disaster, and the Oil Pollution Act of 1990,

216
00:16:34,520 --> 00:16:36,700
came in the form of better tug escorts,

217
00:16:37,040 --> 00:16:39,740
which can tow or nudge the giant tankers away from danger.

218
00:16:39,741 --> 00:16:43,500
GPS systems in concert with improved radar

219
00:16:43,501 --> 00:16:46,800
dramatically improved cleanup technologies

220
00:16:46,801 --> 00:16:52,080
and a mandate that all U.S. tankers have double hulls by 2015.

221
00:16:53,120 --> 00:16:55,620
The accidents have always led to the change of design.

222
00:16:56,200 --> 00:16:58,580
Engineers, we learn from previous screw-ups

223
00:16:58,581 --> 00:17:03,040
and we look very carefully at previous engineering disasters.

224
00:17:06,260 --> 00:17:08,900
While oil spills continue to plague the seas,

225
00:17:08,901 --> 00:17:11,800
at least one region, ironically, is ready.

226
00:17:12,160 --> 00:17:19,140
The irony is probably the only port in the world where you are absolutely prepared for a major oil spill is now Valdez.

227
00:17:20,700 --> 00:17:23,540
If preventing man-made disasters is difficult,

228
00:17:24,240 --> 00:17:29,240
engineering for natural disasters, especially earthquakes, is a mammoth challenge.

229
00:17:31,400 --> 00:17:33,780
According to a U.S. Coast Guard report,

230
00:17:33,781 --> 00:17:38,440
of the 15 double-hull accidents between 1990 and 1999,

231
00:17:38,900 --> 00:17:42,900
None resulted in any oil spilled.

232
00:17:42,901 --> 00:17:46,900
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

233
00:17:46,901 --> 00:17:50,900
We now return to Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

234
00:17:50,901 --> 00:17:56,900
California. Its very name evokes the idea of a promised land.

235
00:17:56,901 --> 00:18:01,900
But the Golden State has always paid a price for its prime location.

236
00:18:01,901 --> 00:18:07,900
Raging wildfires, drought, and giant waves that batter its coast.

237
00:18:07,901 --> 00:18:15,540
Of all the natural disasters that plague California, the deadliest and least understood are earthquakes.

238
00:18:18,560 --> 00:18:23,060
The threat of the big one will always pose a daunting challenge for the state's engineers.

239
00:18:23,820 --> 00:18:26,560
It's not like these hurricanes where you can line up and prepare.

240
00:18:27,180 --> 00:18:30,320
I guess the bright side is we're kind of competing against time.

241
00:18:30,321 --> 00:18:36,720
The longer this big earthquake takes to occur, the better prepared we're getting.

242
00:18:37,900 --> 00:18:44,420
The most painful lessons have taken place at the vulnerable connecting points of California's gigantic circulatory system.

243
00:18:45,980 --> 00:18:47,260
Freeway bridges.

244
00:18:48,740 --> 00:18:53,640
One particularly tragic incident sent shockwaves through the engineering community.

245
00:18:54,480 --> 00:19:02,120
The collapse of the Cypress Viaduct structure in Oakland during the 7.1 Loma Prieta earthquake on October 17, 1989.

246
00:19:02,121 --> 00:19:05,860
The quake lasted 12 seconds

247
00:19:05,861 --> 00:19:09,380
and although the epicenter was 50 miles to the south

248
00:19:09,381 --> 00:19:12,700
the worst structural failure and greatest loss of life

249
00:19:12,701 --> 00:19:13,960
would occur here

250
00:19:13,961 --> 00:19:16,920
when a one and a quarter mile upper deck section

251
00:19:16,921 --> 00:19:18,300
of the 880 freeway

252
00:19:18,301 --> 00:19:20,420
pancaked onto the lower deck

253
00:19:20,421 --> 00:19:22,920
42 people died

254
00:19:22,921 --> 00:19:24,360
crushed in their cars

255
00:19:24,361 --> 00:19:26,560
the detached unstable structure

256
00:19:26,561 --> 00:19:29,160
made rescue efforts difficult and dangerous

257
00:19:29,161 --> 00:19:31,240
It was a progressive collapse

258
00:19:31,241 --> 00:19:36,120
A failure occurs in some localized area, and because of that failure,

259
00:19:36,300 --> 00:19:41,300
the loads then are exceptionally high as they're transmitted to other parts of the structure.

260
00:19:42,680 --> 00:19:46,740
The immediate cause of the disaster was the failure of certain support columns.

261
00:19:47,480 --> 00:19:51,940
The intense horizontal movement of the earthquake caused the columns to shear outward,

262
00:19:52,700 --> 00:19:55,960
leaving the upper roadway no place to go, but straight down.

263
00:19:55,961 --> 00:19:59,980
The top part of the roadway had relatively small columns,

264
00:19:59,981 --> 00:20:04,940
and there were deficiencies right where the columns from the upper level

265
00:20:04,941 --> 00:20:09,980
sat on or essentially met the roadway at the top of the first level.

266
00:20:11,660 --> 00:20:16,180
The column failure demonstrated an especially cruel quirk of earthquake behavior.

267
00:20:16,360 --> 00:20:18,840
Most structures are designed to resist vertical loads.

268
00:20:19,360 --> 00:20:21,940
When you have the earthquake, you're applying lateral loads.

269
00:20:22,480 --> 00:20:26,860
And so for a good earthquake design, you must not only design the structure to take its weight,

270
00:20:26,861 --> 00:20:31,460
but you must design the structure to take these lateral loads that would tend to push it over.

271
00:20:32,840 --> 00:20:35,880
When the Cypress Viaduct was completed in 1957,

272
00:20:36,600 --> 00:20:39,460
it was the state's first double-decked freeway bridge.

273
00:20:40,340 --> 00:20:42,780
Engineers thought they had built in sufficient redundancy

274
00:20:42,781 --> 00:20:45,800
by designing it with two sets of columns instead of one.

275
00:20:46,560 --> 00:20:49,720
But they didn't count on the dramatic increase of destructive force

276
00:20:49,721 --> 00:20:51,400
caused by the ground itself,

277
00:20:51,960 --> 00:20:54,820
a phenomenon known as soft soil amplification.

278
00:20:54,821 --> 00:20:59,540
The cypress structure is located fairly close to the San Francisco Bay,

279
00:21:00,060 --> 00:21:03,440
and the soils underneath it tend to be soft soils.

280
00:21:03,860 --> 00:21:04,780
They call it bay mud.

281
00:21:05,520 --> 00:21:09,700
And at the cypress structure, there's the ground accelerations,

282
00:21:09,880 --> 00:21:12,660
which might have been .05 g, are amplified,

283
00:21:12,980 --> 00:21:16,000
so by the time you hit the foundation of the cypress structure,

284
00:21:16,140 --> 00:21:20,020
there may be .25 g or a five-fold amplification.

285
00:21:21,020 --> 00:21:25,600
G measures the rate of ground acceleration as a percentage of the Earth's gravity.

286
00:21:26,560 --> 00:21:29,960
The Cyprus was built to withstand only 0.06 G.

287
00:21:32,340 --> 00:21:37,500
This flaw stems from the fact that the Cyprus, like many of California's freeway bridges,

288
00:21:38,040 --> 00:21:41,440
was built during a 30-year lull of major earthquake activity.

289
00:21:42,340 --> 00:21:45,000
There simply hadn't been enough big quakes to learn from.

290
00:21:47,120 --> 00:21:49,080
Fast forward to 1994.

291
00:21:50,020 --> 00:21:53,480
the California Department of Transportation, or Caltrans,

292
00:21:53,960 --> 00:21:57,400
began building a new meticulously engineered Cypress Viaduct

293
00:21:57,401 --> 00:21:59,340
in the shadow of its former self.

294
00:22:00,020 --> 00:22:01,980
But just as work was beginning in Oakland,

295
00:22:02,420 --> 00:22:04,720
the 6.6 Northridge earthquake struck,

296
00:22:05,160 --> 00:22:07,700
400 miles to the south in Los Angeles,

297
00:22:08,140 --> 00:22:09,940
where eight freeway bridges collapsed,

298
00:22:10,480 --> 00:22:12,000
some shearing in half,

299
00:22:12,480 --> 00:22:14,220
others simply falling down.

300
00:22:15,840 --> 00:22:17,560
Only the earthquake's timing,

301
00:22:18,100 --> 00:22:19,980
it struck at 4.30 a.m.,

302
00:22:19,981 --> 00:22:21,700
kept freeway fatalities low.

303
00:22:22,820 --> 00:22:24,800
The Northridge quake brought into sharp relief

304
00:22:24,801 --> 00:22:27,340
the urgency of fixing California's freeways.

305
00:22:28,440 --> 00:22:31,540
Back in Oakland, Caltrans engineers and contractors,

306
00:22:32,160 --> 00:22:34,280
once again armed with hard-won knowledge,

307
00:22:34,840 --> 00:22:35,760
continued their work.

308
00:22:36,600 --> 00:22:39,140
The new Cypress structure would be an engineering achievement.

309
00:22:39,820 --> 00:22:43,700
It would be marked by advanced use of a fairly new concept in freeway design,

310
00:22:44,340 --> 00:22:46,500
flexibility, or ductility.

311
00:22:46,501 --> 00:22:49,420
One of the essential features of ductility is that

312
00:22:49,421 --> 00:22:54,780
if you exceed the strength of the structure, it doesn't fail.

313
00:22:54,940 --> 00:22:57,560
It can deform, but it doesn't collapse.

314
00:22:57,561 --> 00:23:02,980
And this is the concept which is now being put into the design of modern freeway structures.

315
00:23:04,240 --> 00:23:07,700
The bend-but-don't-break concept was realized dramatically

316
00:23:07,701 --> 00:23:12,320
in heavily reinforced cast-and-steel shell, concrete piles,

317
00:23:13,040 --> 00:23:15,540
sunk as deep as 90 feet into the bay mud.

318
00:23:19,420 --> 00:23:23,420
and in vastly improved connections where the columns join the roadway.

319
00:23:25,420 --> 00:23:31,420
The new Cypress Viaduct has been designed to withstand 1.8 G of peak horizontal force,

320
00:23:31,421 --> 00:23:34,420
or 30 times greater than its original strength.

321
00:23:34,421 --> 00:23:38,420
It's a model for the future of California's 12,000 miles of freeway

322
00:23:38,421 --> 00:23:44,420
and the type of extreme engineering needed for those who live in the seismic hot zone.

323
00:23:44,421 --> 00:23:50,840
many, many things that have been done to improve the safety of bridges for earthquakes.

324
00:23:51,580 --> 00:23:58,260
And by and large, through a learning process, the bridges are a lot safer in California now

325
00:23:58,261 --> 00:24:01,021
than probably anywhere else in the world.

326
00:24:01,022 --> 00:24:04,320
That doesn't mean we still don't have more to do.

327
00:24:04,980 --> 00:24:07,680
But in the heart of America's coal mining country,

328
00:24:07,681 --> 00:24:12,500
a blatant failure of engineering would be necessary to spark reform.

329
00:24:14,420 --> 00:24:21,040
During the 1995 Kobe Japan quake, a one-third-mile section of expressway collapsed, killing 16 people.

330
00:24:21,640 --> 00:24:26,240
Like the cypress structure, its failure was caused partly by the soft soil on which it was built.

331
00:24:26,820 --> 00:24:28,460
Engineering disasters will return.

332
00:24:29,160 --> 00:24:33,020
We now return to Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

333
00:24:38,240 --> 00:24:43,620
This is Buffalo Creek, West Virginia, a steep, rugged valley in the heart of Appalachia.

334
00:24:44,420 --> 00:24:49,660
The surrounding region of Logan County became known as the birthplace of the Hatfield-McCoy

335
00:24:49,661 --> 00:24:56,240
feud and later as one of the most prolific producers of coal in the United States.

336
00:24:56,241 --> 00:25:03,100
But since February 26, 1972, it's better known as a place torn apart by a dreadful engineering

337
00:25:03,101 --> 00:25:12,400
disaster, one that killed 125 people and forever altered a community.

338
00:25:12,401 --> 00:25:17,160
The roots of the disaster are deeply embedded in the history of coal mining.

339
00:25:17,161 --> 00:25:21,840
Getting coal out of the ground has always been a labor-intensive activity and a dangerous

340
00:25:21,841 --> 00:25:22,840
one.

341
00:25:22,841 --> 00:25:29,460
Methane poisoning, cave-ins, and explosions are just some of the hazards that plague miners.

342
00:25:29,461 --> 00:25:37,080
In 1925 alone, 686 workers died in the mines of West Virginia.

343
00:25:37,081 --> 00:25:43,080
the decades, hard-fought gains in safety conditions and increased automation made coal mining

344
00:25:43,081 --> 00:25:49,080
a somewhat less hazardous job.

345
00:25:49,081 --> 00:25:53,492
But one aspect of the coal mining operation remained dangerously unchecked.

346
00:25:53,493 --> 00:25:54,193
Coal waste

347
00:25:54,194 --> 00:25:54,894
dams.

348
00:25:54,895 --> 00:26:00,520
Unlike traditional dams, coal waste dams provide neither electricity nor a reservoir

349
00:26:00,521 --> 00:26:05,140
of potable water. They are essentially gigantic garbage dumps.

350
00:26:06,760 --> 00:26:12,136
You mine coal, and coal usually has some material in it that's not coal.

351
00:26:12,137 --> 00:26:13,480
You run it through

352
00:26:13,481 --> 00:26:18,292
different processes, and you end up with coal, and then you end up with refuse.

353
00:26:18,293 --> 00:26:18,993
And these

354
00:26:18,994 --> 00:26:21,344
processes include the use of water.

355
00:26:21,345 --> 00:26:24,920
So now you have refuse you have to get rid of, and

356
00:26:24,921 --> 00:26:26,000
water you have to get rid of.

357
00:26:26,001 --> 00:26:32,240
For years, mining companies dumped the wastewater directly into streams, including Buffalo Creek.

358
00:26:32,760 --> 00:26:35,060
But early environmental laws put a stop to that.

359
00:26:37,100 --> 00:26:43,380
The companies were required to at least make an effort to try to control the wastewater from the plant.

360
00:26:44,980 --> 00:26:49,300
The cheapest thing for them to do was just to build a dam across the valley.

361
00:26:49,301 --> 00:26:54,890
In 1960, the Laredo Mining Company built what became known as Dam No.

362
00:26:54,891 --> 00:26:58,940
1, overlooking the narrow valley of Buffalo Creek.

363
00:26:59,660 --> 00:27:07,000
The dam was built of coal waste, also called gob, which smoldered and often caught fire due to its high carbon content.

364
00:27:08,760 --> 00:27:09,460
When No.

365
00:27:09,461 --> 00:27:14,873
1's reservoir became filled with silt, the mining company built the larger dam, No.

366
00:27:14,874 --> 00:27:19,240
2, right behind it, on soft clay soil with little shear strength.

367
00:27:19,300 --> 00:27:22,300
Several near failures plagued the first two dams.

368
00:27:22,301 --> 00:27:29,300
Dam number one overtopped several times and there were black water discharges that came into Buffalo Creek.

369
00:27:29,301 --> 00:27:38,300
Dam number two failed, the front face of it failed and split in the middle a couple of times in the late 60s.

370
00:27:38,301 --> 00:27:39,001
The U.S.

371
00:27:39,002 --> 00:27:44,300
Geological Survey reported that the dams were in fact vulnerable to a washout.

372
00:27:44,301 --> 00:27:52,740
And there was evidence of foundation failure piping in both structures, particularly in dam number two.

373
00:27:53,020 --> 00:27:56,140
But the company went ahead and built the third dam anyway.

374
00:27:57,020 --> 00:28:04,760
Dam number three was to be massive, 500 feet thick in the middle, stretching 465 feet across the valley

375
00:28:04,761 --> 00:28:09,160
and rising 60 feet above dam number two's impoundment pond.

376
00:28:09,161 --> 00:28:21,080
Despite the dam size and the fact that it was to be built overlooking a floodplain home to several small towns, the only engineering plan was this rudimentary sketch.

377
00:28:22,580 --> 00:28:28,420
At the time, engineering requirements for coal dams and federal enforcement were sorely lacking.

378
00:28:29,260 --> 00:28:35,658
There was just one provision that said any refuse embankments and impoundments should be substantially constructed.

379
00:28:35,659 --> 00:28:37,160
That was it. One paragraph.

380
00:28:39,160 --> 00:28:44,820
The mining company's bulldozers began forming the giant face of dam number three in 1968.

381
00:28:46,020 --> 00:28:48,280
The coarse-refuse material was not compacted.

382
00:28:48,420 --> 00:28:53,300
It was dumped by large trucks and then pushed into the center of the valley,

383
00:28:53,400 --> 00:28:57,760
out on top of the slurry that had been discharged by the previous dams.

384
00:28:57,761 --> 00:29:04,260
There's no way one could get compaction with that thick layer of material being pushed forward.

385
00:29:04,640 --> 00:29:07,580
So there was no strength whatsoever in the material.

386
00:29:07,581 --> 00:29:14,420
In addition, the dam was built without proper spillways to alleviate the reservoir when it reached capacity.

387
00:29:16,380 --> 00:29:21,120
Such basic engineering deficiencies made a catastrophe virtually inevitable.

388
00:29:21,900 --> 00:29:28,580
The front face of the dam failed, I believe, in both 1969 and again in 1971.

389
00:29:29,180 --> 00:29:35,680
So there had been two failures of the front face of dam number three prior to February 26, 1972.

390
00:29:35,681 --> 00:29:43,680
anybody with engineering knowledge knew that there were some serious problems with dam number three and its long-term stability.

391
00:29:44,680 --> 00:29:51,800
Despite such evidence, the Pittston Company, the dam's final owners, did not make the needed structural changes.

392
00:29:54,480 --> 00:29:58,240
8 a.m., February 26, 1972.

393
00:29:58,241 --> 00:30:07,580
Four years of runoff from the mine and seasonal rains had swollen the dam's reservoir to 132 million gallons.

394
00:30:08,600 --> 00:30:11,460
Anxious residents noted large cracks in the dam's face.

395
00:30:12,240 --> 00:30:13,540
The crest had become soft.

396
00:30:14,080 --> 00:30:18,720
The mining company officials, even though they had no real engineering knowledge,

397
00:30:19,060 --> 00:30:24,780
took it upon themselves to tell people that there was no danger and they should go home or stay in their homes.

398
00:30:25,640 --> 00:30:31,500
It was only some local residents who actually warned their neighbors and got some people out.

399
00:30:32,300 --> 00:30:39,620
Somebody did call my mother and told her that there was fear, I guess, up on the creek that the dam would break.

400
00:30:40,260 --> 00:30:42,161
And so we kind of knew something was going on.

401
00:30:42,162 --> 00:30:44,600
We didn't have any clue how bad it was going to be, though.

402
00:30:44,601 --> 00:30:54,480
water had saturated so deeply into the dam's interior that a third of its face slid off into

403
00:30:54,481 --> 00:31:00,080
the impoundment pond of dam number two the remainder of the dam then failed catastrophically

404
00:31:00,081 --> 00:31:07,220
it simply liquefied unleashing massive amounts of energy as it overtopped dams one and two

405
00:31:07,221 --> 00:31:12,940
on its unstoppable path to the valley below the flood announced itself with loud explosions

406
00:31:12,941 --> 00:31:16,780
as it slammed into the smoldering gov pile that was dam number one.

407
00:31:17,760 --> 00:31:20,120
The town of Saunders was directly below.

408
00:31:21,000 --> 00:31:23,600
And so within a matter of moments,

409
00:31:23,820 --> 00:31:28,620
the whole town of Saunders was obliterated by this mass of water and slur.

410
00:31:29,340 --> 00:31:34,300
The 20-foot-high flood wave surged with 132 million gallons of water

411
00:31:34,301 --> 00:31:36,520
and 1 million gallons of sludge.

412
00:31:37,060 --> 00:31:40,720
It gained momentum from gravity and from its own increasing mass

413
00:31:40,721 --> 00:31:45,800
as it grabbed up mud, coal waste, and soon, houses and cars.

414
00:31:47,480 --> 00:31:50,700
I could see the creek and I could see all this black water coming down it.

415
00:31:51,440 --> 00:31:54,160
You know, I'm thinking, we got some type of flood.

416
00:31:54,320 --> 00:31:57,080
And then I seen that white house float down and hit the bridge.

417
00:31:57,960 --> 00:32:00,180
And, you know, the house just splintered all the pieces

418
00:32:00,181 --> 00:32:03,080
and the water backed up and started coming over toward where I was at.

419
00:32:04,120 --> 00:32:07,380
As some scrambled up the cold, muddy hillsides to escape,

420
00:32:07,920 --> 00:32:10,300
they looked back to see houses floating by

421
00:32:10,301 --> 00:32:12,680
with people still looking out the windows.

422
00:32:13,580 --> 00:32:17,580
Houses that were soon smashed to bits along overpasses and railroad trestles.

423
00:32:18,220 --> 00:32:21,280
When the trestle started breaking, I heard a lot of people screaming.

424
00:32:22,120 --> 00:32:27,100
And we found out later that there were people on the trestle when it broke.

425
00:32:28,060 --> 00:32:32,200
The torrent of water and waste cut a 17-mile swath of destruction

426
00:32:32,201 --> 00:32:35,000
through the 17 small towns along Buffalo Creek

427
00:32:35,001 --> 00:32:37,200
before joining the Guyon Dot River.

428
00:32:38,040 --> 00:32:40,020
It was all over by 11 a.m.

429
00:32:40,300 --> 00:32:44,040
125 people died.

430
00:32:44,780 --> 00:32:46,100
1,000 more were injured.

431
00:32:47,000 --> 00:32:52,260
507 houses, 44 mobile homes, and 30 businesses were destroyed.

432
00:32:53,380 --> 00:32:55,320
4,000 people were left homeless.

433
00:32:56,040 --> 00:33:01,740
It was like you had civilization one day, and then it was pure chaos the next day.

434
00:33:01,741 --> 00:33:05,400
And that chaos lasted for a very, very, very long time.

435
00:33:06,660 --> 00:33:08,600
Entire families died together.

436
00:33:08,601 --> 00:33:15,160
There was friends and friends of friends and relatives of relatives that died.

437
00:33:17,640 --> 00:33:19,560
A lot of funerals to go to.

438
00:33:24,180 --> 00:33:25,640
Rage followed grief.

439
00:33:26,640 --> 00:33:32,440
Co-operators money and elect you politicians and you get to Washington and you care nothing about us.

440
00:33:32,441 --> 00:33:41,340
As with other disasters of this scale, the Buffalo Creek Dam disaster was a shocking wake-up call to federal and state agencies.

441
00:33:43,000 --> 00:33:52,160
The Army Corps of Engineers and the Mine Safety and Health Administration, or MSHA, launched a comprehensive inspection of coal waste dams across the nation.

442
00:33:52,840 --> 00:34:01,260
We felt that we needed to have an engineering analysis of what was available and be able to tell the mining community,

443
00:34:01,261 --> 00:34:04,460
if you're going to build these things, here's what you've got to do.

444
00:34:05,540 --> 00:34:10,160
Out of the horror of Buffalo Creek came a remarkable era of dam improvement,

445
00:34:10,820 --> 00:34:13,440
where before almost no regulations existed.

446
00:34:14,320 --> 00:34:17,800
MSHA and other agencies wrote detailed engineering specifications.

447
00:34:19,120 --> 00:34:23,720
They demonstrated how proper engineering, combined with vigilant public scrutiny,

448
00:34:24,340 --> 00:34:27,560
can prevent a tragedy like Buffalo Creek from happening again.

449
00:34:28,560 --> 00:34:31,200
Other disasters defy prediction.

450
00:34:31,260 --> 00:34:38,260
and belief. Next, a spectacular chain of catastrophe is unleashed in Louisiana.

451
00:34:38,261 --> 00:34:43,673
There are over 650 coal waste dams in the United States today.

452
00:34:43,674 --> 00:34:49,260
At least 232 are in West Virginia, more than in any other state.

453
00:34:49,261 --> 00:34:54,260
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

454
00:34:54,261 --> 00:35:00,260
We now return to Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

455
00:35:00,261 --> 00:35:05,740
The southern coast of Louisiana, where the water flows, everywhere.

456
00:35:07,120 --> 00:35:15,380
Shallow lakes full of catfish, broad bayous, canals dotted with shrimp boats, all leading to the Gulf of Mexico.

457
00:35:16,520 --> 00:35:24,040
On November 20, 1980, water would play the key role in one of the strangest and most spectacular engineering disasters ever.

458
00:35:24,041 --> 00:35:31,280
This was once the shaft of the Jefferson Island salt mine, where men had been pulling salt

459
00:35:31,281 --> 00:35:32,660
out of the earth since 1919.

460
00:35:32,661 --> 00:35:42,180
Louisiana's salt lies in gigantic underground domes, some larger than Mount Everest, which

461
00:35:42,181 --> 00:35:46,920
are pushed upward by the force of the heavier rock that surrounds them.

462
00:35:46,921 --> 00:35:51,520
Part of the Jefferson Island mine is located directly under Lake Peignore.

463
00:35:51,521 --> 00:35:54,340
The mine, which was operated by the Diamond Crystal Salt Company,

464
00:35:54,940 --> 00:35:59,680
shared space on Jefferson Island with a lush botanical preserve known as Live Oak Gardens.

465
00:36:02,440 --> 00:36:05,400
There's another abundant resource deep under Lake Peñor,

466
00:36:06,040 --> 00:36:08,880
one frequently found in pockets near salt domes.

467
00:36:09,560 --> 00:36:11,560
It's as black as the salt is white.

468
00:36:12,020 --> 00:36:17,800
That salt moves upward and it pierces through surrounding strata,

469
00:36:18,300 --> 00:36:20,720
sands, clays, and limestones.

470
00:36:20,721 --> 00:36:31,000
And this piercing produces faults and folds within these surrounding sediments, producing an ideal mechanism to trap oil.

471
00:36:31,900 --> 00:36:39,860
So it was hardly surprising that Texaco was conducting exploratory drilling close to the Jefferson Island salt mine in November of 1980.

472
00:36:39,861 --> 00:36:46,320
On November 20th, the two delicate underground and underwater operations,

473
00:36:46,980 --> 00:36:51,300
drilling oil and mining salt, collided in spectacular fashion.

474
00:36:53,580 --> 00:36:57,120
Well, it was a typical day in November the 20th, 1980.

475
00:36:57,640 --> 00:37:00,000
A nice, cool, crisp day, clear skies.

476
00:37:00,980 --> 00:37:03,940
People were fishing out on the lake, as they usually do.

477
00:37:05,240 --> 00:37:07,300
The mine was in full operation.

478
00:37:07,301 --> 00:37:13,680
But in the pre-dawn hours, the oil drilling crew on Lake Peignore had run into a problem.

479
00:37:14,660 --> 00:37:16,680
At 500 or 600 feet, they hit salt.

480
00:37:17,180 --> 00:37:21,000
And they were not supposed to hit salt for another couple of hundred feet.

481
00:37:21,900 --> 00:37:23,680
The drill bit became stuck.

482
00:37:24,360 --> 00:37:26,420
The crew heard strange popping sounds.

483
00:37:27,080 --> 00:37:29,320
The oil rig tilted badly to one side.

484
00:37:30,160 --> 00:37:35,580
And at that point in time, the drillers recognized that they had a fairly serious problem on their hands.

485
00:37:35,581 --> 00:37:43,220
What they didn't realize is the 14-inch drill bit had punctured the roof of the salt mine at the 1,300-foot level,

486
00:37:43,920 --> 00:37:46,380
creating a funnel for Lake Pignore to drain into.

487
00:37:47,380 --> 00:37:50,540
It was apparently the result of a simple miscalculation.

488
00:37:51,240 --> 00:37:56,180
It seems that there was an engineering error in the location of the Texaco well.

489
00:37:56,640 --> 00:38:00,180
They used a form of triangulation for determining the location,

490
00:38:00,181 --> 00:38:05,380
and one of the points they used for triangulating was an error.

491
00:38:05,580 --> 00:38:13,860
And consequently, it rotated the location of the borehole about 400 feet closer to the main mine than it should have been.

492
00:38:14,280 --> 00:38:19,900
90 minutes after the drill became stuck, the drilling crew, who had wisely abandoned the tilting rig,

493
00:38:20,540 --> 00:38:28,180
watched in amazement from the shore as their 150-foot derrick disappeared into a lake that was less than 10 feet deep.

494
00:38:28,181 --> 00:38:39,180
Meanwhile, 1,300 feet below the surface of Lake Peñor, diamond crystal miners, who had not been alerted to a problem, looked up to see something you should never see

495
00:38:39,181 --> 00:38:40,180
in a salt mine.

496
00:38:40,900 --> 00:38:41,600
Water.

497
00:38:41,740 --> 00:38:45,040
Water flowing through salt tends to dissolve the salt.

498
00:38:46,120 --> 00:38:51,680
As you increase the volume of water, you increase the volume of salt that can be dissolved.

499
00:38:53,240 --> 00:38:58,120
Soon it was clear. Lake Peñor was draining through the hole bored by the drill bit.

500
00:38:58,180 --> 00:38:59,720
and into the salt mine.

501
00:39:00,800 --> 00:39:04,640
As the 2.5 billion gallons of Lake Peneur surged into the mine

502
00:39:04,641 --> 00:39:06,860
with 10 times the pressure of a fire hydrant,

503
00:39:07,440 --> 00:39:09,840
a mighty whirlpool was forming on the surface.

504
00:39:12,160 --> 00:39:14,980
Remarkably, thanks to well-rehearsed evacuation drills

505
00:39:14,981 --> 00:39:16,540
and the heroics of several miners,

506
00:39:17,040 --> 00:39:19,980
all 55 people in the mine managed to get out.

507
00:39:20,760 --> 00:39:24,240
But back on the surface, the chaos was just beginning.

508
00:39:28,180 --> 00:39:37,260
The whirlpool is basically a vortex of water and mud that is flowing down into a fairly confined space.

509
00:39:37,261 --> 00:39:45,240
And that whirlpool is basically flowing downwards under the force of gravity, pulling sediment and other materials down with it.

510
00:39:45,820 --> 00:39:51,520
The whirlpool sucking force pulled heavy-duty barges toward it as their crews leapt for safety.

511
00:39:51,521 --> 00:39:58,400
men watched with jaws agape as 11 barges and a tugboat disappeared into the vortex

512
00:39:58,401 --> 00:40:05,020
those are four fully loaded flatbed trucks spinning helplessly in the whirlpool

513
00:40:05,021 --> 00:40:13,360
enter leon's viator jr who had simply wanted to catch some fish that day i was crazy enough to be

514
00:40:13,361 --> 00:40:21,220
on the lake november the 20th 1980 so i asked my nephew to come with me on the boat and was

515
00:40:21,221 --> 00:40:28,220
gonna go catch some catfish. We went. We're gonna go and try our luck.

516
00:40:28,221 --> 00:40:32,220
What his luck would have it that day.

517
00:40:32,221 --> 00:40:38,220
Vietor soon found himself and his 14-foot boat being yanked toward the whirlpool and certain death.

518
00:40:38,221 --> 00:40:47,220
When we got further down on the lake, I spotted something, an eye in the ground.

519
00:40:47,221 --> 00:40:53,380
to me it measured 100 feet in diameter and there was a lot of barges all glooped to it

520
00:40:54,420 --> 00:41:00,500
i didn't know what was happening when a barge that was blocking the way was sucked into the hole

521
00:41:00,501 --> 00:41:05,860
it gave viator a narrow path to skirt the maelstrom he gunned the motor and reached the

522
00:41:05,861 --> 00:41:12,740
shore with seconds to spare viator then tied his boat to a tree when i looked like this i seen my

523
00:41:12,741 --> 00:41:17,285
boat that I had tied to the tree and there it was going into the whirlpool.

524
00:41:17,286 --> 00:41:18,740
So that's the last thing

525
00:41:18,741 --> 00:41:23,603
I've seen, my boat tied up to the tree going into the hole.

526
00:41:23,604 --> 00:41:26,900
By that point, the whirlpool was sucking

527
00:41:26,901 --> 00:41:28,794
down Jefferson Island itself.

528
00:41:28,795 --> 00:41:33,300
Before evening, 65 acres of land, including much of live oak gardens,

529
00:41:33,301 --> 00:41:38,700
would end up at the bottom of the mine. 150 year old trees were snapped in half.

530
00:41:38,701 --> 00:41:39,780
There were pecan

531
00:41:39,781 --> 00:41:44,520
trees that were 150 feet tall down here in this woods and to stand there and

532
00:41:44,521 --> 00:41:50,460
watch them drop completely out of sight yeah along the shoreline along the

533
00:41:50,461 --> 00:41:54,960
live old gardens and the Jefferson Island mansion you're basically seeing

534
00:41:54,961 --> 00:41:59,700
what are called landslides where the ground is actually shearing and breaking

535
00:41:59,701 --> 00:42:04,740
apart and gravity is pulling these blocks down into the hole

536
00:42:09,780 --> 00:42:14,960
That house that we were standing on, all you see now is a chimney.

537
00:42:15,560 --> 00:42:17,100
The house sank about 30 feet.

538
00:42:18,380 --> 00:42:19,420
Could things get worse?

539
00:42:20,320 --> 00:42:21,020
Yes.

540
00:42:21,720 --> 00:42:25,960
Delcum Canal, which normally flowed away from Lake Peñor and into the Gulf of Mexico,

541
00:42:26,700 --> 00:42:30,220
actually reversed direction under the intense sucking power of the whirlpool.

542
00:42:31,040 --> 00:42:34,160
As the water rushed into the now nearly empty lake bed,

543
00:42:34,161 --> 00:42:39,880
it formed a 150 foot waterfall, the highest ever in Louisiana.

544
00:42:41,880 --> 00:42:46,040
But that's the only time the Gulf of Mexico flew north, basically.

545
00:42:46,740 --> 00:42:49,800
So that's a lot of water to fill up that hole.

546
00:42:51,080 --> 00:42:53,140
As the water rushed into the mine,

547
00:42:53,640 --> 00:42:56,300
its rapid displacement of the underground caverns

548
00:42:56,301 --> 00:42:58,400
shot a deafening blast of compressed air

549
00:42:58,401 --> 00:43:02,420
and later a 400 foot geyser up from the mine shaft.

550
00:43:02,421 --> 00:43:05,440
To see something like this, it was terrible, really.

551
00:43:05,920 --> 00:43:08,240
I thought it was the end of the world.

552
00:43:09,340 --> 00:43:12,880
It took two days for the canal's water to fill up the mine in Lake Peignore.

553
00:43:13,620 --> 00:43:19,400
When the water pressure equalized, nine of the 11 sunk barges popped up like corks in a bathtub.

554
00:43:21,640 --> 00:43:25,540
The drilling operation and the underground salt mine were complete losses.

555
00:43:27,860 --> 00:43:30,620
The cause of the disaster was never officially determined,

556
00:43:30,621 --> 00:43:34,980
since all the evidence was at the bottom of a 1,500-foot water-filled salt mine.

557
00:43:35,880 --> 00:43:40,580
But there's no definitive evidence that the mine itself was structurally unsound.

558
00:43:41,720 --> 00:43:46,080
The culprit was most likely a 14-inch wide drill bit.

559
00:43:48,700 --> 00:43:52,360
This engineering disaster did have at least one positive effect.

560
00:43:52,980 --> 00:43:55,840
It made Lake Peneur a little more interesting.

561
00:43:55,841 --> 00:44:01,080
It's a shallow lake and now we have part of it that's a deep lake.

562
00:44:01,081 --> 00:44:04,640
So it's changed the type of fish that live in the lake.

563
00:44:04,641 --> 00:44:10,620
And we catch a redfish and saltwater species that were not very prevalent here before.

564
00:44:10,621 --> 00:44:14,780
But not everyone is planning a fishing trip to Lake Benyur.

565
00:44:14,781 --> 00:44:22,900
What I went through and got out of there alive, I don't care for it really.

566
00:44:22,901 --> 00:44:25,160
I prefer buying my fish.

567
00:44:27,600 --> 00:44:31,760
It can hardly be said that the Jefferson Island disaster had a happy ending.

568
00:44:32,780 --> 00:44:36,260
The loss to livelihoods and cherished property was devastating.

569
00:44:37,080 --> 00:44:40,680
Yet the fact that no human beings perished was fortunate indeed.

570
00:44:41,540 --> 00:44:44,620
For if there's one lesson to take from this strange engineering disaster,

571
00:44:45,380 --> 00:44:48,800
it's that a small mistake can have huge consequences.

572
00:44:48,801 --> 00:44:54,840
Think the first Superdome was opened in 1965?

573
00:44:55,360 --> 00:44:57,140
You're about 2,000 years off.

574
00:44:57,380 --> 00:45:00,160
Think the first gated communities were built in the 1980s?

575
00:45:00,320 --> 00:45:01,020
Think again.

576
00:45:01,180 --> 00:45:04,220
The Romans, they not only conquered the ancient world,

577
00:45:04,460 --> 00:45:05,880
they conquered the future.

578
00:45:06,340 --> 00:45:08,360
Rome, engineering and empire.

579
00:45:08,680 --> 00:45:09,380
Tonight at 8th.

